#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069674} :activity/id #uuid "045e390c-f9e0-5c62-a1d5-1f73b3832910" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050497 :topic/id #uuid "4dd99b27-25f2-551e-b641-cafc64c1d0b4" :topic/math-academy-id 393} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070037 :tutorial/content "To solve a two-term quadratic equation like $x^2 - 7x = 0$ where there is no constant term, we can factor out the variable and apply the zero-product rule.\n\nFactoring out the variable, we have:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tx^2 - 7x &= 0 \\\n\tx(x - 7) &= 0\n}\n$$\n\nWe apply the zero product rule to get \n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x-7=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives \n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=0,7.$\n\nTo check that these solutions are correct, we can substitute them back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =0 & \\, & \\, & x & =7 \\\\  & ⇓ &  &  &  & ⇓ \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x & =0 &  &  & {x}^{2}-7x & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}-7(0) & =0 &  &  & {7}^{2}-7(7) & =0 \\\\ 0-0 & =0 &  &  & 49-49 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓ &  &  & 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Warning:** It's tempting to solve the equation by dividing by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-7x}{x} & =\\frac{0}{x} \\\\ x-7 & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we do that, we lose the solution $x=0.$ So, when solving equations, *never divide by a variable* because the variable could be zero, and division by zero is not allowed. Instead, try to factor the variable in the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "bda6c872-00e7-50b0-b4bb-685694b60283" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9238 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c8c690e8-98b4-5d35-95ce-fc393db9c622" :step/math-academy-id 193672 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068765 :knowledge-point/